\newtheorem{theorem}{Theorem}\newtheorem{lemma}[theorem]{Lemma}
\title{Thirteen hexagons through every edge: exclusion of order thirty-three}
\author{Human Compression project}\date{}
\begin{document}\maketitle
\begin{theorem}\label{main}
A finite simple quartic graph of girth six with exactly thirteen hexagons
through every edge has at least thirty-six vertices.
\end{theorem}
This recovers the lower bound in the $\lambda=13$ row of Table~2,
physical page~18, of Goedgebeur--Jooken, \emph{Exhaustive generation of
edge-girth-regular graphs}, arXiv:2401.08271v2, 25 June 2024. The row
records the improved bound $36$.
No existence assertion at order thirty-six is made.

\section{Seven independent outside vertices}
Fix an edge $uv$. The two rooted trees of depth two obtained by excluding
$uv$ at their first step have twenty-six distinct vertices. Girth six
excludes identifications and extra edges within a side. The only additional
edges among the twenty-six vertices join opposite leaves. Each side has
three branches, each with three leaves; the edges between a pair of
opposite branches form a matching. A cross edge gives exactly one hexagon
through $uv$, and every such hexagon arises this way. Thus there are
thirteen cross edges. Write their nine matching sizes as $3-d_{ij}$; then
\[
       0\leq d_{ij}\leq3,\qquad \sum_{i,j=0}^2d_{ij}=14.        \tag{1}
\]
There are fourteen missing leaf incidences on each side. They must all meet
the outside set $Y$, since any further cross edge changes the count on $uv$.
If $t=|Y|$, summing degrees in $Y$ gives
\[
                         2e(Y)=4t-28.                         \tag{2}
\]
Hence $t\geq7$. Moreover, the total number of hexagons is $13n/3$, so $3\mid n$. To prove the theorem, it remains only to exclude $n=33$, or $t=7$.

\begin{lemma}\label{outside}
At order thirty-three, $Y$ consists of seven independent vertices. Each has
four neighbours among the Moore leaves, between one and three on each
side. For some integer $a$ with $0\leq a\leq3$, each side has $a$ outside
vertices with one neighbour, $7-2a$ with two, and $a$ with three.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Equation (2) gives independence, so all four neighbours of every outside
vertex are leaves. Two neighbours in the same branch create a quadrangle;
there are only three branches per side. Thus the two side degrees are
positive, at most three, and complementary to four. On either side their
sum is fourteen. If $n_i$ counts outside vertices with side degree $i$, then
$n_1+n_2+n_3=7$ and $n_1+2n_2+3n_3=14$, giving $n_1=n_3=a$.
The complementary side degrees have the same distribution.
\end{proof}

We state the local counting equations used in the finite proof. Let
$P_L,P_R$ be the positive missing leaf degrees on the two sides, each
summing to fourteen, and put
\[
                    f(P)=\frac{42-\sum_{s\in P}s^2}{2}.       \tag{3}
\]
There are thirteen hexagons through $u$ which avoid $uv$: the four incident
edges account for twenty-six hexagons through $u$, of which thirteen use $uv$.
A right leaf with missing degree $s$ has $3-s$ cross neighbours in distinct
left branches, supplying $\binom{3-s}{2}$ of the former hexagons.
Subtracting these old contributions shows that the outside vertices must
supply $f(P_R)$ pairs of left leaves. Interchanging sides gives $f(P_L)$.
Lemma~\ref{outside} therefore implies the useful necessary condition
\[
                  f(P_L)=f(P_R)=7+a\in\{7,8,9,10\}.             \tag{4}
\]
This equality is a restriction on possible completions, not a claim that
a degree profile determines the outside graph.

The earlier incidence-partition capacity bound is not used in this
component. Condition (4), together with the exact individual equations
below, supplies the restrictions needed here.

Write $L_0,L_1,L_2$ for the left leaf branches and $a_i$ for the number of
cross edges meeting $L_i$. The individual equations are
\[
            \sum_w |N(w)\cap L_i|\,|N(w)\cap L_j|=a_k
            \quad (\{i,j,k\}=\{0,1,2\}),                    \tag{5}
\]
once the leaves have been saturated. Indeed, if $b_{ij}$ counts hexagons
through the two root edges in branches $i,j$, then
$a_i+b_{ij}+b_{ik}=13$ and $a_0+a_1+a_2=13$, whence $b_{ij}=a_k$.
Their paths are simple by girth six, so common neighbours of the leaves
count exactly these hexagons. The same equations hold on the right.

\section{Complete enumeration of the Moore configurations}
Both the input constructor and the verifier independently enumerate all
integer matrices (1). There are $30\,276$ matrices and $492$ orbits under
row and column permutations and transpose. These transformations are
actual symmetries of the two rooted trees.

For each representative use the acquired theorem on partial matchings
along a forest. The six branch leaf sets are its vertices. In a forest
block of matching size $k$, directed from parent to child, retain each
parent $k$-subset matched in order to the first $k$ child labels. A fresh
child permutation puts any matching in this form and acts on all blocks
incident with that child. Previously normalized forest blocks remain
fixed. Induction away from the forest roots proves coverage. A reverse
orientation uses the transposed prescription; every block outside the
forest retains all partial injective matchings. This gives an orbit
cover, without asserting that representatives are unique.

The chosen forests yield $19\,656\,978$ normalized product choices.
The traversal rejects partial choices only for a newly created
quadrangle; the graph at this stage is bipartite. At a complete choice
it tests (4). Reuse of a retained root graph requires a literal
bijection preserving the unordered root edge and all adjacency rows.
Nauty supplies only a lookup suggestion and a proposed bijection. A false
merge fails this check; a false split merely leaves another case to process.
The completed traversal has $17\,170\,921$ prefixes and $12\,703\,806$
girth-admissible assignments. Its $2\,252\,232$ checked maps retain
$102\,701$ root graphs.

\section{Attaching the right incidences to known vertices}
For each root graph, first partition its fourteen left incidences according
to their outside neighbour. Insert every incidence into each permissible
existing part or a new part, naming parts by first appearance. Require
exactly seven nonempty parts, each of size at most three. A repeated leaf,
two leaves of the same branch, an old common neighbour, or a repeated
leaf pair gives a quadrangle and is rejected. The new contributions to
(5) are nonnegative; a partial sum may not exceed its required value,
and at the end equality is required. These tests are all necessary.
Every possible graph therefore supplies at least one enumerated partition.

Repeated incidences of the same left leaf are processed in increasing
part-index order. This removes permutations of indistinguishable copies.
To see completeness, take any final partition, order its parts by their
least leaf and break ties arbitrarily. Process the leaves in increasing
order and their parts in that order. Parts whose least leaf has already
occurred exist when selected; all other parts are created at this step,
in the prescribed order. The resulting traversal realizes the partition
up to outside-vertex relabelling. Every possible right attachment is
subsequently tried for these retained labels, so this relabelling loses
no graph. It need not preserve the number of named partitions.

There is also an exact size restriction at every partial partition. Put
$a=f(P_R)-7$. A part already of size three cannot shrink, and a part
already of size at least two cannot become a singleton. Therefore there
may be at most $a$ current parts of size three and at most $7-a$ current
parts of size at least two. Reject also when the remaining incidences
cannot create enough new parts to reach seven. These tests discard only
partial partitions which cannot have the final distribution in
Lemma~\ref{outside}.

Name its seven parts $0,\ldots,6$, install the corresponding seven outside
vertices and all their left edges, and give vertex $j$ the residual
capacity $4-|B_j|$. There are no other outside vertices or outside edges
by Lemma~\ref{outside}. It remains to attach the fourteen right incidences to
these seven fixed vertices. Process the incidences grouped by right leaf,
and try every outside vertex with positive residual capacity. Repeated
incidences of the same leaf choose strictly increasing outside labels.
This loses no graph: they describe an unordered set of distinct neighbours,
which has a unique increasing ordering.

Before adding an edge, require its endpoints to have current distance
at least five. An existing shorter path would create a cycle of length
at most five, while a missing such path makes the addition safe. This
check includes paths through outside vertices already installed. Track
the right-hand equations (5), rejecting a repeated branch within one
outside vertex or a contribution exceeding its target. When all fourteen
incidences are attached, require equality in all three equations and
zero residual capacities. Every edge and every vertex of the resulting
graph is now present. There is no implicit completion step.

This coupled recursion enumerates the same potential completions as
forming right partitions independently and then trying every bijection
whose paired part sizes sum to four. It avoids forming the entire product
of the two partition lists. Its correctness does not depend on any
observed run time. Every move restores the adjacency rows, capacities
and pair counts when the recursion returns.

The completed run examines all $102\,701$ retained roots. Its left
search has $378\,647\,435$ nodes and $21\,278\,612$ accepted partitions,
arising at $37\,630$ roots. The right search has $31\,114\,357$ nodes.
No complete quartic graph of girth six survives, even before the final
thirteen-hexagon test. An earlier traversal without ordering repeated
left incidences reached its declared time limit and supplies no coverage.

As an implementation check, the actual old and new left visitors are run
on both sides of thirteen selected roots. Their complete sets of sorted
part masks agree in all twenty-six cases, including four nonempty cases:
$43\,728$ old named partitions and $4\,042$ new named partitions describe
the same $884$ part families. The literal partial-size guard is also
compared with an independent injection of current parts into the seven
required final sizes; all $452$ partial size profiles agree.

To test that the actual incidence visitor admits valid completions, a
control starts with the already checked graph of order thirty-two with
eighteen hexagons per edge. For every subset of its nine right-to-outside
edges, remove that subset and run the same visitor with the resulting
capacities and literal pair targets. All $512$ cases recover exactly
one complete graph. A separate program chooses entire neighbour subsets
for each deficient right vertex, uses no intermediate cycle or pair-count
pruning, and checks the completed graphs independently. Its $24\,136$
terminal candidates give the same $512$ answers. These are implementation
controls, not substitutes for the complete target enumeration.

\begin{proof}[Proof of Theorem~\ref{main}]
Equation (2) excludes orders below thirty-three, and hexagon counting
requires $3\mid n$. At order thirty-three, Lemma~\ref{outside} applies.
The forest traversal covers the Moore configuration of every hypothetical
graph. Every reuse has a checked literal isomorphism; all prunes express
necessary conditions. Its left outside partition is retained, and the
coupled right recursion includes all its remaining edges. The complete finite record contains no such graph. Thus order thirty-three is excluded, and divisibility excludes thirty-four and thirty-five.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Attribution and scope.}
The lower bound $36$ is attributed to the stated source row. The accepted
fifteen-hexagon component supplies the independent-exterior argument,
profile equations and coupled incidence recursion; the partial-matching
forest theorem and literal-map verification are also acquired. This
component adapts those arguments to seven exterior vertices, adds the
hexagon divisibility step, the exact partial-size test and the ordering of repeated left incidences, and supplies
fresh target computations. The author also authored the acquired
fifteen-, eighteen- and nineteen-hexagon components. A separate reviewer
checked the seven-vertex reduction in advance; that preliminary read is
not the final finite-certificate review. No priority, newly solved
conjecture, whole-source, Lean or compression-factor claim is made.
